  "query_preview": "..Very warm temperatures for this time of year will\ncontinue over the next several days with tomorrow being the\nwarmest of this stretch. Highs in the mid to upper 80s will\nchallenge daily records across the area. The KSLC record for the\nday tomorrow is 87. The high today was 86, and with slightly lower\nheights and cloud cover increase ahead of a weak shortwave it will\nbe a tall task to challenge the record.\n\nThis weak shortwave will move through on Tuesday. Decent moisture\nwill be available with PWATs between 0.55-0.65\", but the forcing\nwill be lacking. This will result in isolated showers and\nthunderstorms late Monday through Tuesday, primarily in the\nmountains as they will act as the primary forcing mechanism.\nTemperatures behind this wave will remain well above normal in the\nlow 80s (low to mid 90s for St. George) for most valley locations\nthrough the remainder of the week.\n\nBy next weekend the pattern will start to change at our first\nsignificant trough of the season is poised to move through the\nwestern CONUS. There are a lot of moving parts with this setup as\nthere will be tropical moisture from the remnants of Hurricane\nRachel in the eastern Pacific that could get advected into the\nincoming trough. The uncertainty with the track of Rachel and the\ntiming of the trough have ensemble members posing quite a range of\nsolutions from an earlier trough passage (~59% of members) with\nless moisture to a delayed trough passage (~41% of members) with\nmore moisture. Additionally, a few ensemble members are also\nbringing in more of a continental airmass from interior Canada\nthat doesn't phase with the tropical moisture and keeps most of\nthe moisture east of the Continental Divide. All of this happens\nsome time late this weekend into early next week. The most certain\naspect of the forecast will be a significant drop in temperatures\nwith a cold front that will be associated with the trough.",
